{
  "doc": "The Home Assistant integration's contract with Logger (logger_app, github.com/sgtpepperpt/ha-logger-app; docs/home-assistant.md): one example answer of every route it reads — GET /about, GET /status?tz=, GET /quick (one entry of each kind), POST /quick/<id>/fire (201, and a med tick's 409 with `already`), POST /shopping (201, and 202 = queued while Nextcloud is down), POST /tablet/wake (200, and 409 = not set up), GET /alerts — keyed by HTTP status where a route has two. Rules + scenes (F39, `about.features` has \"rules\"): `ha_catalog.request` = the body the integration PUTs to /ha/catalog (Logger checks it is accepted), `ha_actions` = GET /ha/actions (the integration's every poll: waiting actions to run + the revs it refetches /ha/rules?kind=from_ha and /ha/scenes on), `ha_action_result` = POST /ha/actions/<id>/result, `ha_rules` = GET /ha/rules?kind=from_ha (HA-side triggers to register; a `device` one carries the trigger config as HA listed it), `ha_rule_fire` = POST /ha/rules/<id>/fire (an HA trigger happened: Logger runs the rule's Logger-side actions itself), `ha_rule_state` = POST /ha/rules/<id>/state (registration outcome), `ha_scenes` = GET /ha/scenes, `ha_webhook` = the body Logger POSTs to the integration's webhook (a nudge, never data). backend/tests/test_ha_contract.py checks every key path here against what the server answers, with the same JSON type (null = the key is nullable); the integration's tests parse a copy. A key the server adds never breaks the integration and never bumps `about.api`; a key it reads renamed, removed or retyped does both. `status.food` = status_parity.json's first case (25 Oct 13:30Z, Lisbon). `status.changes` is keyed by data: one key per kind written so far (food, body, blood, journal, meds, goals, pressure, workouts, tasks). Null means unknown, never 0: no calorie goal, no medication today, Tasks off. Instants are ISO 8601 with an offset; the caller's `tz` decides every day.",
